questions.stem:
An orchard sensor bands overnight readings, and its two named bands do not cover every value. What does the printer output?
int temp = 15;
String band;
if (temp < 0) {
    band = "FREEZE";
} else if (temp >= 20) {
    band = "HIGH";
} else {
    band = "MILD";
}
System.out.println(band);

A driving-school pass mark has two bands that both accept 55, and the ladder is written in the order shown. What prints?
int score = 55;
String band;
if (score >= 50) {
    band = "PASS";
} else if (score >= 40) {
    band = "MARGINAL";
} else {
    band = "FAIL";
}
System.out.println(band);

A sign-builder wants HUGE to beat POSITIVE for values above 100, but the broad test is written first. What actually happens?
int n = 12;
String kind;
if (n > 0) {
    kind = "POSITIVE";
} else if (n > 100) {
    kind = "HUGE";
} else {
    kind = "OTHER";
}
System.out.println(kind);

A triage tool switches on an enum but lists only two of the three constants, with no default label. What prints?
enum Level { LOW, MEDIUM, HIGH }
Level given = Level.HIGH;
String note = "unclassified";
switch (given) {
    case LOW:
        note = "routine";
        break;
    case MEDIUM:
        note = "watch";
        break;
}
System.out.println(note);

An age gate rejects minors, and the rejection counter is bumped inside the guard before the return. What prints?
int[] attempts = {0};
boolean admit(int age) {
    if (age < 18) {
        attempts[0]++;
        return false;
    }
    return true;
}
boolean r1 = admit(15);
boolean r2 = admit(20);
System.out.println(attempts[0] + " " + r1 + " " + r2);

Each pass of a loop adds 100 after the switch, so the running total reveals exactly how many passes complete. What prints?
int total = 0;
for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
    switch (i) {
        case 2:
            total += 10;
            break;
        default:
            total += 1;
    }
    total += 100;
}
System.out.println(total);

An arrow-form switch expression builds a label; the third case is a block, so it produces its value with yield. What prints?
int code = 3;
String label = switch (code) {
    case 1 -> "one";
    case 2 -> "two";
    case 3 -> {
        String s = "th";
        yield s + "ree";
    }
    default -> "many";
};
System.out.println(label);

A thermostat bands readings with two inclusive upper bounds. A switch cannot hold a range, so a chain is the right shape here. What prints?
int temp = 72;
String band;
if (temp <= 32) {
    band = "COLD";
} else if (temp <= 80) {
    band = "WARM";
} else {
    band = "HOT";
}
System.out.println(band);

A vending tariff first collapses the volume to a band number, then switches on that number. What prints?
int litres = 9;
String price;
switch (litres / 5) {
    case 0:
        price = "small";
        break;
    case 1:
        price = "medium";
        break;
    default:
        price = "large";
        break;
}
System.out.println(price);

A ticket kiosk bands age in a chain, then subdivides the adult band with a second chain inside the else. What prints?
int age = 25;
boolean member = true;
String price;
if (age < 13) {
    price = "child";
} else {
    if (member) {
        price = "member";
    } else {
        price = "standard";
    }
}
System.out.println(price);

Three two-way branches each add a different amount, and only the bodies that actually run contribute. How many marks are added with a true, b false, c true?
boolean a = true;
boolean b = false;
boolean c = true;
int marks = 0;
if (a) {
    marks = marks + 1;
} else {
    marks = marks + 10;
}
if (b) {
    marks = marks + 100;
}
if (c) {
    marks = marks + 1000;
}

A guard was meant to stop a zero divisor, but it was written after the division it should prevent. What happens?
int half(int n) {
    int result = 100 / n;
    if (n == 0) {
        return -1;
    }
    return result;
}
System.out.println(half(0));

Two adjacent bands meet at a shared edge, one written strictly and the other inclusively. Which band does 10 land in?
int x = 10;
String band;
if (x < 10) {
    band = "SMALL";
} else if (x <= 10) {
    band = "EXACTLY_TEN";
} else {
    band = "LARGE";
}
System.out.println(band);

A band labelled EXACTLY_TEN is meant to catch the value 10, but the inclusive test is written above the strict one. What happens?
int x = 10;
String band;
if (x <= 10) {
    band = "SMALL";
} else if (x < 10) {
    band = "EXACTLY_TEN";
} else {
    band = "LARGE";
}
System.out.println(band);

Two candidate band tests are compared over the readings -1, 3 and 8. Which statement about them is true?
boolean r1 = v < 0 || v > 2;
boolean r2 = v < 5 && v > 2;

A loyalty classifier lists a compound test first; for this customer two of the bands both match. Which offer is stored?
int age = 20;
int visits = 3;
String offer;
if (age >= 18 && visits >= 3) {
    offer = "LOYAL";
} else if (age >= 18) {
    offer = "ADULT";
} else if (visits >= 3) {
    offer = "REGULAR";
} else {
    offer = "NEW";
}
System.out.println(offer);

A three-level classifier is missing its top band, and the catch-all reuses the bottom band's label. What does level 3 print?
int level = 3;
String band;
if (level == 1) {
    band = "LOW";
} else if (level == 2) {
    band = "MID";
} else {
    band = "LOW";
}
System.out.println(band);

Each continue skips one sample that belongs to a band. How many samples reach the final increment?
int[] samples = {-2, 0, 5, 12, 13, 40};
int defaults = 0;
for (int s : samples) {
    if (s < 0) {
        continue;
    }
    if (s <= 10) {
        continue;
    }
    if (s <= 20) {
        continue;
    }
    defaults++;
}
System.out.println(defaults);

For v equal to 15, which of these five chains actually run their final else? Select every chain that does.
A: if (v < 10) band = "L"; else if (v < 20) band = "M"; else band = "H";
B: if (v < 10) band = "L"; else band = "M";
C: if (v > 20) band = "L"; else if (v > 5) band = "M"; else band = "H";
D: if (v >= 15) band = "L"; else if (v > 5) band = "M"; else band = "H";
E: if (v < 0 || v > 30) band = "L"; else band = "M";

A case label may be any compile-time constant expression, and the first label equal to the selector runs. What prints?
int base = 2;
int v = 6;
String name;
switch (v / base) {
    case 3:
        name = "THREE";
        break;
    case 3 + 1:
        name = "FOUR";
        break;
    default:
        name = "NONE";
}
System.out.println(name);

A grading rule awards A at 90 and up, B from 70, and F below that. Fill the blank so a score of 75 is awarded B.
int score = 75;
char grade;
if (score >= 90) {
    grade = 'A';
} else if (score ____) {
    grade = 'B';
} else {
    grade = 'F';
}
System.out.println(grade);

A payment status is a small set of fixed codes, and the code already switches on the code itself. Why is a switch the better fit than an else-if chain here?
int code = 2;
String name;
switch (code) {
    case 1:
        name = "PAID";
        break;
    case 2:
        name = "UNPAID";
        break;
    default:
        name = "UNKNOWN";
}

A compound test is listed first, so the single-test band below it is only reachable for values the compound rejects. What prints for 120?
int x = 120;
String s;
if (x > 0 && x < 100) {
    s = "IN_RANGE";
} else if (x > 0) {
    s = "POSITIVE";
} else {
    s = "OTHER";
}
System.out.println(s);

Three guards run in the order written, so a validity check and a policy check compete for the same booking. What does plan(80, false) return?
static String plan(int seats, boolean paid) {
    if (seats <= 0) {
        return "NO SEATS";
    }
    if (!paid) {
        return "UNPAID";
    }
    if (seats > 50) {
        return "BULK";
    }
    return "STANDARD";
}
System.out.println(plan(80, false));

The author meant to stop as soon as the code was unrecognised, but wrote break where a return was needed. What does convert(9) give?
static int convert(int c) {
    int out = 0;
    switch (c) {
        case 1:
            out = 1;
            break;
        default:
            break;
    }
    out = out + 5;
    return out;
}
System.out.println(convert(9));

This chain has no final else, so the label it was given beforehand is the real default. What prints for 420?
int n = 420;
String label = "UNKNOWN";
if (n < 10) {
    label = "SMALL";
} else if (n < 100) {
    label = "MEDIUM";
}
System.out.println(label);

One threshold is written twice in this five-branch classifier. How many of the five branches can ever be selected?
if (n > 100) s = "A";
else if (n > 10) s = "B";
else if (n > 5) s = "C";
else if (n > 100) s = "D";
else s = "E";

The rule is BRONZE below 10, SILVER from 10 up to but not including 25, and GOLD from 25 up. Fill the blank so the classifier matches that rule.
int coins = 12;
String band;
if (coins < 10) {
    band = "BRONZE";
} else if (coins < ____) {
    band = "SILVER";
} else {
    band = "GOLD";
}
System.out.println(band);

Two seasonal bands each need two comparisons joined by and, so every branch tests a compound expression rather than one fixed value. Which construct fits?
if (month >= 3 && month <= 5) {
    season = "SPRING";
} else if (month >= 6 && month <= 8) {
    season = "SUMMER";
} else {
    season = "OTHER";
}
